    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Мы с Димой обсудили дела.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Dima and I discussed the affairs.' Here 'to discuss' uses the plural past-tense ending, which also serves for the polite 'you'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "У меня есть хорошая идея.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I have a good idea.' Notice the word order: 'me' comes first, and 'idea' finishes the sentence.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Как сделать ресторан лучше.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'How to make the restaurant better.' Here 'how' sets up the idea, while 'better' comes at the end for emphasis.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Но сначала хочу поговорить с вами.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'But first I want to talk with you.' You'll hear 'first' all the time in the show, here connected with 'you'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Только с вами, Виктор Петрович.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Only with you, Viktor Petrovich.' The first content word means 'only', and 'Petrovich' rounds out the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Если вы не против, Виктория Сергеевна?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'If you do not mind, Victoria Sergeyevna?' In Russian, 'you' leads into 'Sergeyevna' in a very natural everyday phrasing. Vika answers straight away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Да, конечно, я не против.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes, of course, I do not mind.' Vika puts 'of course' up front so the listener immediately catches the tone, followed by 'I'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "dima",
      "t": "Замечательно, я вас слушаю.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Great, I am listening to you.' In Russian, 'wonderful' leads into 'I'm listening' in a very natural everyday phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я посмотрела зарплаты сотрудников.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I looked at the employees salaries.' In Russian, 'I' leads into 'staff members' in a very natural everyday phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Нам нужно сделать увольнения.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We need to make layoffs.' A compact expression built around 'us' and 'dismissal'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "dima",
      "t": "О чём вы говорите?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What are you talking about?' Notice the word order: 'what' comes first, and 'say' finishes the sentence.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Вика получает очень много денег.",
